# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ArchGuard environment variables
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Two things to know before you start.
#
# `.env` is loaded by the *application* (archguard/dashboard/app.py), not by
# pytest and not by scripts/dev_services.py. Anything the tests need must be
# exported into your shell -- see docs/DEVELOPMENT.md, which explains why a
# populated `.env` still leaves every database test silently skipped.
#
# The production gate in archguard/dashboard/_config_check.py is the authority
# on what is required. It runs when ENVIRONMENT=production and refuses to serve
# traffic rather than starting in a weaker state. Everything it checks is marked
# REQUIRED (production) below.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# ── Required in production ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Each of these stops a production deployment from booting if it is missing or
# wrong. In development they are optional and the gate does not run.

# SESSION_SECRET — REQUIRED (production). The HMAC key for session cookies.
# Generate: python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_hex(32))"
#
# Deliberately NOT the same value as ARCHGUARD_DASHBOARD_TOKEN, and there is no
# fallback to it. They were one secret before, which meant rotating the operator
# credential signed every user out, and anyone who learned it could forge any
# session. Rotating this one invalidates every outstanding session, which is
# exactly what it is for. The gate also refuses a value under 32 characters.
SESSION_SECRET=

# GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID / GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET — REQUIRED (production).
# Users sign in with GitHub. Register an OAuth app at
# https://github.com/settings/developers with the callback URL
# <your-origin>/auth/github/callback. Only read:user is requested: repositories
# are cloned anonymously over public HTTPS and ArchGuard never acts on a user's
# behalf.
#
# Without these there is no way to sign in, so every data endpoint answers 401.
# Their absence also enables the loopback development fallback, which is why
# production refuses to start without them.
GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=

# DATABASE_URL — REQUIRED (production). The +asyncpg driver is not optional:
# SQLAlchemy would otherwise pick a synchronous driver and deadlock the event
# loop. Platforms that inject a bare postgres:// URL are handled automatically.
# Users, jobs, runs, findings, suppressions and watches all live here.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://archguard:CHANGE_ME@127.0.0.1:5432/archguard_dev

# REDIS_URL — REQUIRED (production). Sessions, rate-limit counters, job progress
# and the analysis queue. The web process enqueues here and the worker consumes.
# Without it analyses run inside the web process, which is a development
# convenience the production gate refuses.
REDIS_URL=redis://127.0.0.1:6379/0

# ALLOWED_ORIGINS — REQUIRED (production). Comma-separated browser origins.
# The gate refuses an unset value in production, and refuses "*" outright:
# credentials are sent with these requests, so a wildcard would let any site
# read a signed-in user's analyses.
ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000,http://localhost:8000,http://127.0.0.1:8000

# ARCHGUARD_TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS — REQUIRED (production).
# Behind a proxy every request otherwise looks like it came from the proxy, so
# all users share one rate-limit bucket and one client can lock out the service,
# including the endpoints that cost money per call. Set to the proxy's CIDR, or
# "*" when the platform guarantees it is the only ingress (Railway, Render).
ARCHGUARD_TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS=

# ENVIRONMENT — the switch that arms everything above. Set to "production" and
# the startup gate runs, session cookies gain Secure, and HSTS is sent. Any
# other value, including unset, and none of that happens.
ENVIRONMENT=development


# ── Authentication, beyond the required set ──────────────────────────────────

# ARCHGUARD_DASHBOARD_TOKEN — Optional. An operator credential, not a user
# login: it identifies nobody, so it reads no user's data. Signed-in users are
# unaffected by its absence -- their session authenticates them on its own.
# What it provides is API access with no browser, and the signing key for the
# short-lived SSE stream tokens. The gate refuses it only if it equals
# SESSION_SECRET.
# ARCHGUARD_DASHBOARD_TOKEN=

# ARCHGUARD_DASHBOARD_ALLOW_REMOTE — DANGER, and a production boot failure.
# Allows unauthenticated access from non-loopback addresses. The gate refuses to
# start when this is enabled in production; it exists for local experiments only.
# ARCHGUARD_DASHBOARD_ALLOW_REMOTE=

# GITHUB_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI — Optional. Set only when the public origin differs
# from what the app sees. Behind a proxy that terminates TLS the app builds an
# http:// callback for an https:// site and GitHub rejects the mismatch.
# GITHUB_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=https://your-domain/auth/github/callback

# ARCHGUARD_TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS — Optional, default: 1.
# How many trusted proxies sit between the client and this process. The client
# IP is read as the Nth X-Forwarded-For entry counting from the RIGHT, because a
# proxy only appends what it saw and never validates entries already there -- so
# every entry further left is chosen by the caller. Set to 2 if a CDN sits in
# front of your platform proxy. Too high is safe (users share a bucket); too low
# is not.
# ARCHGUARD_TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=1

# ARCHGUARD_SESSION_COOKIE_TTL — Optional, default: 86400 (24 hours).
# How long a signed-in session lasts. Stored in Redis with this TTL.
# ARCHGUARD_SESSION_COOKIE_TTL=86400


# ── GitHub API ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# GITHUB_TOKEN — Optional but strongly recommended.
# Read when fetching repository metadata. Without it the GitHub API allows 60
# requests/hour unauthenticated; a classic personal access token raises that to
# 5000. Minimum scope for public repositories: public_repo.
GITHUB_TOKEN=


# ── LLM / AI (all optional; analysis runs without any of it) ─────────────────

# GEMINI_API_KEY — Optional. Powers the AI Advisor and AI remediation plans.
# Without it those two features report themselves unavailable; the four analysis
# layers are unaffected. Get one at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# OPENAI_API_KEY — DEPRECATED alias for GEMINI_API_KEY. Read only when
# GEMINI_API_KEY is unset, so existing deployments keep working; it must still
# contain a *Gemini* key. Warns when used.
# OPENAI_API_KEY=

# GEMINI_MODEL — Optional, default: gemini-3.6-flash.
# The model every AI call uses, and the one the boot check below verifies.
# There is no second model and no fallback: ARCHGUARD_PRIMARY_MODEL and
# ARCHGUARD_FALLBACK_MODEL were removed because nothing ever called them --
# they were verified at boot and reported by /api/v1/capabilities while every
# request used this one, so a typo here passed the check and failed at runtime.
# GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-3.6-flash

# GEMINI_BASE_URL — Optional. ArchGuard talks to Gemini over its
# OpenAI-compatible endpoint; override only for a proxy or a different version.
# GEMINI_BASE_URL=https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/openai

# ARCHGUARD_VERIFY_LLM_ON_BOOT — Optional, default: 0.
# Ask the Gemini API at startup whether the configured models exist, and log
# loudly if they do not. Worth turning on wherever the ids are not the shipped
# defaults: a wrong id fails every AI call with a message that reads like a bad
# credential, so an operator checks their key and finds nothing wrong with it.
# Costs one request at boot, which is why it is off.
ARCHGUARD_VERIFY_LLM_ON_BOOT=0

# ARCHGUARD_MOCK_LLM — Optional. Returns canned LLM responses instead of calling
# the API. Used by the test suites; never set it in a deployment.
# ARCHGUARD_MOCK_LLM=1

# ARCHGUARD_REMEDIATION_MAX_TOKENS — Optional, default: 4096.
# Ceiling on one remediation-plan completion.
# ARCHGUARD_REMEDIATION_MAX_TOKENS=4096


# ── Worker and queue ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ARCHGUARD_WORKER_CONCURRENCY — Optional, default: 2.
# How many analyses one worker runs at once. A memory budget rather than a
# throughput knob: each holds a full clone on disk and, with layers 3 and 4
# enabled, a loaded embedding model. Scale out with more workers instead.
# ARCHGUARD_WORKER_CONCURRENCY=2

# ARCHGUARD_JOB_TIMEOUT — Optional, default: 900 (seconds).
# Outer bound on one queued analysis including the clone. The analysis itself is
# capped separately by ARCHGUARD_ANALYSIS_TIMEOUT.
# ARCHGUARD_JOB_TIMEOUT=900

# ARCHGUARD_INLINE_ANALYSIS — development only.
# Forces analyses to run in the web process even when a queue is configured, for
# attaching a debugger. Never set this in a deployment: it puts untrusted
# repository parsing back into the process holding every session key.
# ARCHGUARD_INLINE_ANALYSIS=1

# ARCHGUARD_REDIS_TIMEOUT — Optional, default: 5 (seconds).
# Connect and socket timeout for Redis. A hung Redis must not hang a request.
# ARCHGUARD_REDIS_TIMEOUT=5


# ── Data retention ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ARCHGUARD_RETENTION_DAYS — Optional, default: 90.
# How long a completed analysis run and its findings are kept. A nightly sweep
# on the worker deletes anything older, along with jobs that have finished and
# have no runs left, and file hashes for repositories nothing refers to any
# more. An unfinished job is never touched at any age, and watched repositories
# and suppressions never expire -- they are configuration, not history.
# ARCHGUARD_RETENTION_DAYS=90

# ARCHGUARD_RETENTION_BATCH — Optional, default: 500.
# Rows per sweep, so one transaction cannot try to delete a year of history at
# once. A backlog drains over successive nights.
# ARCHGUARD_RETENTION_BATCH=500


# ── Analysis behaviour and limits ────────────────────────────────────────────

# ARCHGUARD_SKIP_ML — Optional. Set to "1" to skip Layers 3 and 4, which need
# the ML extras. Faster, and reports both layers as not measured rather than as
# clean. Note for developers: with this set, the Layer 3/4 tests do not exercise
# the code they are about -- see docs/DEVELOPMENT.md.
# ARCHGUARD_SKIP_ML=1

# ARCHGUARD_DASHBOARD_PROFILE — Optional, default: "ci".
# Threshold preset for contracts the dashboard generates for repositories that
# ship no .archguard.yml. "ci" allows a coupling fan-out of 10; "strict" (5)
# flags healthy libraries and "lenient" (15) lets tangled ones through.
# ARCHGUARD_DASHBOARD_PROFILE=ci

# ARCHGUARD_CLONE_TIMEOUT — Optional, default: 120 (seconds).
ARCHGUARD_CLONE_TIMEOUT=120

# ARCHGUARD_ANALYSIS_TIMEOUT — Optional, default: 600 (seconds).
ARCHGUARD_ANALYSIS_TIMEOUT=600

# ARCHGUARD_EVOLUTION_TIMEOUT — Optional, default: 300 (seconds).
# Bound on one git-history analysis. Each commit it examines creates a worktree
# and runs a full four-layer analysis; without a bound, a repository whose
# commits are slow to analyse pinned a thread until the client gave up.
# ARCHGUARD_EVOLUTION_TIMEOUT=300

# ARCHGUARD_PIP_AUDIT_TIMEOUT — Optional, default: 60 (seconds).
# Bound on the dependency vulnerability scan, which runs pip-audit as a
# subprocess in the worker and reaches the network.
# ARCHGUARD_PIP_AUDIT_TIMEOUT=60

# ARCHGUARD_STREAM_IDLE_LIMIT — Optional, default: 300 (seconds).
# How long an SSE progress stream may sit with no event before it is closed, so
# an abandoned browser tab does not hold a connection open indefinitely.
# ARCHGUARD_STREAM_IDLE_LIMIT=300

# ARCHGUARD_EMBEDDING_BATCH_SIZE — Optional, default: 500.
# ARCHGUARD_LOCK_TIMEOUT — Optional, default: 30.0 (seconds).
# ARCHGUARD_MAX_REPO_SIZE_KB — Optional, default: 512000 (500 MB). Submissions
#   above this are refused with 413 before anything is cloned; 0 disables it.
# ARCHGUARD_MAX_WORKSPACE_BYTES — Optional, default: 5368709120 (5 GiB). Ceiling
#   on all analysis workspaces together; the oldest finished ones are evicted
#   until the total fits. A finished job's clone is only a cache. 0 disables it.


# ── Rate limiting ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ARCHGUARD_RATE_LIMIT_MAX_REQUESTS — Optional, default: 50 per minute per client.
# ARCHGUARD_LLM_RATE_LIMIT_MAX_REQUESTS — Optional, default: 30.
# Tuned for one person browsing. A browser test suite drives dozens of page
# loads from one address and exhausts them, after which every later page fails
# to load its own auth status -- which looks like a flaky selector rather than a
# rate limit. Raise them for test runs only. The counters live in Redis and are
# shared across processes, so two servers on one machine share a budget.
# ARCHGUARD_RATE_LIMIT_MAX_REQUESTS=50
# ARCHGUARD_LLM_RATE_LIMIT_MAX_REQUESTS=30


# ── Storage, logging and observability ───────────────────────────────────────

# ARCHGUARD_DATA_DIR — Optional, default: .archguard-cache
# Where the audit log lives. The production gate probe-writes it and refuses to
# start if it is not writable, because the audit logger swallows its own write
# failures by design -- a root-owned volume under a non-root container would
# otherwise fail silently for the life of the deployment.
# ARCHGUARD_DATA_DIR=.archguard-cache

# LOG_LEVEL — Optional, default: INFO. Accepts the standard Python level names.
# The application logs one JSON object per line. WARNING is a good choice for a
# test run: at INFO the per-request lines can fill a captured pipe.
# LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# ARCHGUARD_DB_POOL_SIZE / ARCHGUARD_DB_MAX_OVERFLOW — Optional, 5 / 10.
# Set the pool size to 0 to disable pooling entirely, which is what the tests do.
# ARCHGUARD_DB_ECHO — Optional. Set to 1 to log every SQL statement.

# SENTRY_DSN — Optional. Unhandled exceptions are reported when set. sentry-sdk
# is not a dependency: it is imported only if this is set, so a deployment that
# does not want it installs nothing. Session cookies, Authorization headers and
# OAuth codes are scrubbed before anything is sent.
# SENTRY_DSN=

# SENTRY_TRACES_SAMPLE_RATE — Optional, default: 0.0. Errors are always
# reported; traces are the expensive part, and 0 is right for a service whose
# slowest operation is deliberately slow.
# SENTRY_TRACES_SAMPLE_RATE=0.0

# ARCHGUARD_RELEASE — Optional. Tags Sentry reports with a release identifier.
# ARCHGUARD_RELEASE=


# ── Audit log ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ARCHGUARD_AUDIT_SECRET — Optional. HMAC key for audit-log entries. When unset,
# a random key is generated once and stored beside the log at 0600; there is no
# hardcoded default. Set this explicitly to verify entries elsewhere, or to keep
# the key off a disk that other processes can read.
# ARCHGUARD_AUDIT_SECRET=

# ARCHGUARD_AUDIT_STRICT — Optional. Set to "1" to refuse to log rather than
# generate a key, for deployments that require the key to be supplied.
# ARCHGUARD_AUDIT_STRICT=1

# ARCHGUARD_AUDIT_MAX_SIZE_MB — Optional, default: 10.
# ARCHGUARD_AUDIT_MAX_ENTRIES — Optional, default: 1000.


# ── Tests only ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read by the test suite, never by the application.

# TEST_DATABASE_URL — the integration tests migrate this database up and tear it
# back down to base. Point it at a SEPARATE database: aiming it at your
# development one drops your data. It must be EXPORTED into your shell -- pytest
# does not read this file. Unset, every database test skips.
TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://archguard:CHANGE_ME@127.0.0.1:5432/archguard_test

# PLAYWRIGHT_REUSE_SERVER — set to 1 when you started the dashboard yourself,
# so Playwright uses it instead of launching its own. See docs/DEVELOPMENT.md
# for what that means for the environment the browser suites expect.
